  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nAs CAA ceases this evening with high pressure ridging and drier air \nmoving in, expect the last of the lake effect rain showers to end \nover the eastern U.P. tonight. While there could be a changeover to \nsnow at times over the north central and east the rest of this \nafternoon and evening as temperatures continue to gradually cool \n(melting layer is practically now at the sfc), no accumulations are \nexpected given the warm ground and (mostly) above freezing \ntemperatures expected across the area the rest of today through \ntonight. Expect the remaining liquid to be received across the north \nwind belts today through this evening to be less than a tenth of an \ninch. \n\nDrier weather returns this weekend and looks to stay in place across \nUpper MI through at least early next week. With drier air being \nadvected in from the Southern Plains, we could see elevated fire \nweather concerns back across the area this weekend as gusty \nsouthwest winds in association with a LLJ return to the area and RHs \npotentially drop into the 20 percents. However, given the recent \nrainfall and cooler temperatures expected (particularly on Saturday \nwhere highs are only projected to get into the 50s), this risk is \nexpected to be fairly mitigated. That being said, with the ongoing \ndrought concerns still to deal with as well as fallen leaves at the \nsfc, elevated fire weather concerns could be realized by Sunday \nacross the west half of Upper Michigan.\n\nFrom Monday onwards next week, fairly quiet weather is expected \nacross the area. While we may have a cold front skirt us and bring \nsome rain showers overhead around Wednesday/Thursday, with more high \npressure ridging building into the central CONUS by the end of next \nweek, we may remain dry for the rest of the forecast period.",
